{"id":"gustav-iv-adolf","order":3055,"name":"Gustav IV Adolf","region":"europe-west","region_label":"Western Europe","realm":"Monarch of Sweden","era":"early-modern","era_label":"Early Modern","blurb":null,"reign_from":1792,"reign_to":1809,"year_from":1792,"year_to":1809,"wp":"Gustav IV Adolf","source":"wikidata","civ_wiki":null,"sources":{"wikidata":"ok","wikipedia":"ok"},"qid":"Q52929","wikidata_url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q52929","wd_label":"Gustav IV Adolf of Sweden","wd_description":"King of Sweden between 1792-1809 (1778–1837)","birth_year":1778,"death_year":1837,"reign_wikidata":[1792,1809],"image":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Gustav_IV_Adolf_%28Johann_Baptist_Lampi_d.%C3%A4.%29_-_Nationalmuseum_-_37526.tif?width=640","family_qid":"Q3276433","predecessor_qid":null,"successor_qid":null,"father_qid":"Q52930","mother_qid":"Q234311","children_qids":["Q472635","Q241254","Q268382","Q641581","Q24018143"],"birthplace_qid":"Q1754","deathplace_qid":"Q25607","position_qids":["Q1268572"],"family":"House of Holstein-Gottorp","predecessor":null,"successor":null,"predecessor_id":null,"successor_id":null,"father":"Gustav III of Sweden","father_id":"gustav-iii","mother":"Sophia Magdalena of Denmark","children":["Gustav, Prince of Vasa","Princess Sophie of Sweden","Princess Cecilia of Sweden","Princess Amalia of Sweden","Charles-Gustave de Suède"],"birthplace":"Stockholm","birthplace_coord":[18.06861,59.32944],"deathplace":"St. Gallen","deathplace_coord":[9.37722,47.42333],"positions":["Monarch of Sweden"],"extract":"Gustav IV Adolf or Gustav IV Adolph (1 November 1778 – 7 February 1837) was King of Sweden from 1792 until he was deposed in a coup in 1809. He was also the last Swedish monarch to be the ruler of Finland.\nThe occupation of Finland in 1808–09 by Russian forces was the immediate cause of Gustav Adolf's overthrow, violently initiated by officers of his own army. Following his abdication on 29 March 1809, an Instrument of Government was hastily written, which severely circumscribed the powers of the monarchy. The \"Instrument\" was adopted in 1809 on 6 June, the National Day of Sweden now as well as in his time. It remained in force until replaced in 1974. The crown, now with strictly limited powers, passed to Gustav Adolf's uncle Charles XIII.","wp_description":"King of Sweden from 1792 to 1809","wikipedia_url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustav_IV_Adolf","image_lead":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Gustav_IV_Adolf_of_Sweden.jpg","thumbnail":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/Gustav_IV_Adolf_of_Sweden.jpg","chat_ready":true,"display_from":1792,"display_to":1809}